rather than just throwing it at chat GPT and expecting it to, as you said, generate random numbers, for example.

230
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:52,471
which it's not going to be going to be good at. Right.

231
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:54,230
Exactly.

232
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:59,509
It's always very surprising what capabilities AI models have and don't have.

233
00:14:59,509 --> 00:15:01,990
Sometimes I ask a model to oneshot an entire page of code.

234
00:15:01,990 --> 00:15:06,550
that I've been thinking about and it just gets it done perfectly on the first try.

235
00:15:06,550 --> 00:15:12,390
But there's other very simple actions that models can't do unless you give it the tools to do. so.

236
00:15:12,399 --> 00:15:14,389
Absolutely.

237
00:15:14,389 --> 00:15:16,470
I've attempted generate system just to see what would happen.

238
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:22,389
And you wouldn't be surprised that it's just a big mess by giving it. that much all at once. Then of course there's the opposite end of the spectrum.

239
00:15:22,389 --> 00:15:24,230
How many bees are in blueberry?

240
00:15:24,230 --> 00:15:27,751
But you know when you consider token size and things of that nature, people get bent at a shape.

241
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:29,509
Oh, the AI can't tell you that.

242
00:15:29,509 --> 00:15:31,110
Well, it's a tool.

243
00:15:31,110 --> 00:15:33,990
It may not be able to do that, but it's really good at a bunch of other things.

244
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,150
Exactly.

245
00:15:36,150 --> 00:15:41,750
And you can always use tool calls to address the weakness of a foundation model.

246
00:15:41,750 --> 00:15:46,949
If you give it a Python script that says, "Hey, do this substring matching problem."

247
00:15:46,949 --> 00:15:49,110
That's a very simple Python script to implement,

248
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:52,470
it can write that script for you and then run that script for you.

249
00:15:52,470 --> 00:15:54,629
and then tell you how many Ps are in Blueberry.

250
00:15:54,639 --> 00:16:00,310
You just need to give it the access to the tools in the runtime environment to do that.

251
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:02,231
Yeah. Interesting.

252
00:16:02,231 --> 00:16:03,749
So, there's a few directions we can go,

253
00:16:03,749 --> 00:16:09,030
but I want to start with let's say we've gotten this Dungeons.

254
00:16:09,030 --> 00:16:11,110
and Dragons AI agent to a certain point.

255
00:16:11,110 --> 00:16:15,990
but everything we've done so far, we're using off-the-shelf models.

256
00:16:15,990 --> 00:16:18,550
When would you start to make that transition and what would you do?

257
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:27,830
Yeah, so there's a few places where I see AI teams start to run into some of the. limitations of off-the-shelf models.

258
00:16:27,839 --> 00:16:31,829
A big one is domain specific quality.

259
00:16:31,829 --> 00:16:34,629
So when we think about our system,

260
00:16:34,629 --> 00:16:41,350
we are trying to for example have a fun role- playinging voice.

261
00:16:41,350 --> 00:16:43,829
where it's going to be adventurous and exciting.

262
00:16:43,829 --> 00:16:48,069
and engaging in the same way that say a book or movie dialogue would be.

263
00:16:48,069 --> 00:16:53,670
Your average off-the-shelf model does not have very much personality like that.

264
00:16:53,670 --> 00:16:56,230
You can get a long way with prompting,

265
00:16:56,230 --> 00:16:59,829
but you can always tell when some text is written by a certain model.

266
00:16:59,839 --> 00:17:02,629
Mh.

267
00:17:02,629 --> 00:17:05,029
So, this is even more important, of course, in in real world systems.

268
00:17:05,029 --> 00:17:09,350
I work with a lot of companies in the health care space.

269
00:17:09,350 --> 00:17:15,429
where they're investing a ton of money in training and fine-tuning models that do better.

270
00:17:15,429 --> 00:17:17,029
and do better enough.

271
00:17:17,029 --> 00:17:21,270
that they can actually be used in the real world for both front and back office healthcare things.

272
00:17:21,270 --> 00:17:24,550
It's important in finance. It's important in education.

273
00:17:24,559 --> 00:17:29,430
There are all these places where you want to train.

274
00:17:29,430 --> 00:17:31,990
or fine-tune a model so that it performs better in your domain.

275
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:38,230
So that's that's the first place is domain specific quality makes people start looking.

276
00:17:38,230 --> 00:17:40,789
The second reason people start looking is latency.

277
00:17:40,789 --> 00:17:46,230
We're now talking about four or five models running.

278
00:17:46,230 --> 00:17:53,270
and sometimes running multiple calls each in between every single user action.

279
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:55,750
So, I ask the system, you know, hey, my guy swung his sword.

280
00:17:55,750 --> 00:17:58,789
Do you hit the hydra?

281
00:17:58,789 --> 00:18:00,710
And if it's waiting four or 5 seconds to tell me the answer to that,

282
00:18:00,720 --> 00:18:02,950
it's not a very good player experience.

283
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:08,950
So, latency is important in literally every single part of AI.

284
00:18:08,950 --> 00:18:13,190
It's especially important in voice AI when people are speaking to a system in real time.

285
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:14,870
And then the third thing is economics.

286
00:18:14,870 --> 00:18:21,110
Like if I'm making this app and it is blowing up.

287
00:18:21,110 --> 00:18:24,150
and going viral and I am paying linearly more in token costs.

288
00:18:24,150 --> 00:18:27,510
for every single user who signs up and start using it.

289
00:18:27,510 --> 00:18:29,270
it's going to get really expensive really quick.

290
00:18:29,270 --> 00:18:31,990
Of course, there's a ton of money in the AI space.

291
00:18:31,990 --> 00:18:36,310
I can always raise around and and just continue to gain market share.

292
00:18:36,310 --> 00:18:40,070
But at some point, I'm gonna have to start thinking about the unit economics of my product.

293
00:18:40,070 --> 00:18:46,070
and making sure that it makes sense and that I'm operating a sustainable business in the long term.

294
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:51,842
What's interesting is those three factors you just mentioned were true before everybody started adopting AI.

295
00:18:51,852 --> 00:18:52,390
[laughter]

296
00:18:52,400 --> 00:18:55,270
It's it sounds like it's just software engineering.

297
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:57,270
That's true.

298
00:18:57,270 --> 00:18:59,590
That's maybe if you take nothing else away from this episode is.

299
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:04,950
that AI engineering is just software engineering with some new tricks to it.

300
00:19:04,960 --> 00:19:12,230
I think a perfect example of this is we see.

301
00:19:12,230 --> 00:19:14,390
so many times where we have a model that we've optimized.

302
00:19:14,390 --> 00:19:20,070
we've shaved every single millisecond off of the prefill and the decode and all these inference steps.

303
00:19:20,070 --> 00:19:24,950
We've put it up on some autoscaling GPU clusters, colllocated it next to the customer.

304
00:19:24,950 --> 00:19:28,950
You know, we've gotten everything perfect down to the milliseconds and we're like,

305
00:19:28,950 --> 00:19:31,029
"Hey, the benchmark for this is 200 milliseconds."

306
00:19:31,039 --> 00:19:35,270
and they come back to us and say, "Dude, I'm seeing 250 300 in production."

307
00:19:35,270 --> 00:19:37,751
Like, what's going on? It's the client code.

308
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:44,230
It's just session reuse or it's some pass through,

309
00:19:44,230 --> 00:19:50,230
some redirect proxy that's adding a few dozen milliseconds here or there.

310
00:19:50,230 --> 00:19:52,549
So there's so many times when the problems.

311
00:19:52,549 --> 00:19:58,310
that we run into in production around these AI systems are less related to the model itself.

312
00:19:58,310 --> 00:20:00,549
or the inference service.

313
00:20:00,549 --> 00:20:02,870
or any of the cool stuff that I like to talk about.

314
00:20:02,870 --> 00:20:11,430
and just pure ordinary software engineering that still needs to be done to an incredibly high degree of. quality and craft in this new industry.

315
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:15,029
It makes sense and you talked about multiple steps.

316
00:20:15,029 --> 00:20:21,830
For example, if I have an agent that's orchestrating a five-step process and those steps can't be parallelized,

317
00:20:21,830 --> 00:20:23,510
well, any one step can slow the entire.

318
00:20:23,510 --> 00:20:25,029
It doesn't matter if four out of five steps.

319
00:20:25,029 --> 00:20:28,630
or 20 milliseconds if one of the steps is 2,000, for example.

320
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:31,351
Exactly. Yeah. So, we're right back to that.

321
00:20:31,351 --> 00:20:34,870
So, with these systems, let's switch gears a little bit.

322
00:20:34,870 --> 00:20:39,029
There's obviously a lot of information passing back and forth now between the human and these systems.

323
00:20:39,029 --> 00:20:43,110
If we're talking about Dungeons and Dragons and in a game,

324
00:20:43,110 --> 00:20:47,510
maybe the risk isn't super high of nefarious things happening with our data.

325
00:20:47,510 --> 00:20:52,710
But the more we hand off capabilities to an agent, seems like trust becomes more and more important.

326
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:59,510
So speak some to how do we handle issues around sensitive data.

327
00:20:59,510 --> 00:21:03,430
or around agents that maybe have power that is a little dangerous to be handing them.

328
00:21:03,430 --> 00:21:10,390
You know, I think of the agent I saw the other day that did a rmspace-rf slash.

329
00:21:10,390 --> 00:21:12,149
And fortunately, it had the right path afterwards,

330
00:21:12,149 --> 00:21:15,190
but I sort of had that moment of, "Oh my gosh, what if it just was slash?

331
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:16,950
I would have been in big trouble.

332
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:19,430
Absolutely.

333
00:21:19,430 --> 00:21:22,549
So, there's a few different things here.

334
00:21:22,549 --> 00:21:24,630
I think number one is just making sure.

335
00:21:24,630 --> 00:21:27,110
that you understand the tools you're giving the agent access to.

336
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:29,750
You can choose what tools it has.

337
00:21:29,750 --> 00:21:34,149
if it doesn't have access to a given tool that it can't take that action.

338
00:21:34,149 --> 00:21:40,470
The second thing is human in the loop for more sensitive actions.

339
00:21:40,470 --> 00:21:43,350
So let's say I was making a stock trading agent.

340
00:21:43,350 --> 00:21:50,230
that was going to manage my portfolio on my behalf.

341
00:21:50,230 --> 00:21:55,510
Maybe I want to have it text me every time it's going to make a trade for more. than $1,000. so that I can just give it a quick thumbs up.

342
00:21:55,510 --> 00:21:58,470
and make sure it's not doing something dumb.

343
00:21:58,470 --> 00:22:01,990
The final thing is, you know, more on the eval side,

344
00:22:01,990 --> 00:22:04,710
like making sure that we're rigorously testing our models.

345
00:22:04,710 --> 00:22:09,270
making sure that we're evaluating our models on the actual domain.

346
00:22:09,270 --> 00:22:11,270
that we're going to be deploying them in.

347
00:22:11,270 --> 00:22:15,669
rather than just looking at the launch blog post and being like,

348
00:22:15,669 --> 00:22:19,271
"Wow, this has a higher version number than the last model, so it must be better." and just,

349
00:22:19,271 --> 00:22:19,272
[laughter]

350
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:21,750
cowboying it straight into production.

351
00:22:21,760 --> 00:22:24,630
So, it's a multi-step process.

352
00:22:24,630 --> 00:22:29,029
It's having a clear understanding of what good looks like,

353
00:22:29,029 --> 00:22:33,510
having some safeguards in place around what systems you're giving it access to at all.

354
00:22:33,510 --> 00:22:38,630
and then having that sort of yellow light system of the human in the loop to prevent any.

355
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:42,149
kind of nefarious actions.

356
00:22:42,149 --> 00:22:46,630
You know, in general, I find myself pretty trusting of AI agents.

357
00:22:46,630 --> 00:22:51,510
The folks working on alignment are doing a pretty good job these days.

358
00:22:51,510 --> 00:22:56,470
So I think of it more around the idea of preventing mistakes rather than preventing nefarious behavior.

359
00:22:56,470 --> 00:22:59,830
For nefarious behavior that that's still coming from humans.

360
00:22:59,830 --> 00:23:01,990
You know, we have all the same problems around,

361
00:23:01,990 --> 00:23:05,830
you know, with base 10, we got a lot of GPUs people sign up try.

362
00:23:05,830 --> 00:23:08,870
and like crypto mine and stuff.

363
00:23:08,870 --> 00:23:13,190
So there's all of the same human nefarious problems that you have to solve in this agent world.

364
00:23:13,190 --> 00:23:19,830
And then you have to add in the safeguards against mistakes from the AI system as well.

365
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:23,510
So two terms you mentioned I want to make sure we define for listeners.

366
00:23:23,510 --> 00:23:25,350
Evals and alignment.

