00:23:25,350 --> 00:23:28,470
Can you describe what those terms mean in case they're unfamiliar to listeners?

368
00:23:28,480 --> 00:23:31,669
Absolutely.

369
00:23:31,669 --> 00:23:34,149
So an eval is a quality benchmark.

370
00:23:34,149 --> 00:23:35,990
Generally it's used in the industry to mean one.

371
00:23:35,990 --> 00:23:41,909
that you've created yourself or one that you've created with your own product in mind.

372
00:23:41,909 --> 00:23:46,710
So, an example of an eval for a D&amp;D system would be,

373
00:23:46,720 --> 00:23:55,990
let's say you have a model that is deciding what level of monster to give your characters.

374
00:23:55,990 --> 00:23:58,630
and you want to make sure it's evenly matched.

375
00:23:58,630 --> 00:24:02,630
So, you might make a set of good party setups.

376
00:24:02,630 --> 00:24:09,909
and a set of good monsters and make sure that the model is able to match them appropriately.

377
00:24:09,909 --> 00:24:13,029
That's an example of a domain specific eval for alignment.

378
00:24:13,039 --> 00:24:18,230
That's more of a philosophical question in the AI industry of making sure.

379
00:24:18,230 --> 00:24:23,830
that our models are prompted and are constrained and and fine-tuned.

380
00:24:23,830 --> 00:24:27,269
to be helpful, to be harmless, to be useful.

381
00:24:27,279 --> 00:24:31,590
So that's, you know, making sure that the models aren't swearing,

382
00:24:31,590 --> 00:24:37,751
aren't creating negative images, all that kind of stuff. And they're related problems.

383
00:24:37,760 --> 00:24:43,350
You can have evals for alignment but yeah generally the models.

384
00:24:43,350 --> 00:24:48,070
that get released to the public these days are quite well aligned.

385
00:24:48,070 --> 00:24:54,630
They are very rigorously tested and we've seen them be you know very very useful in general.

386
00:24:54,630 --> 00:24:58,549
So that's why I said you know alignment is pretty good these days.

387
00:24:58,559 --> 00:25:01,350
Yeah.

388
00:25:01,350 --> 00:25:06,789
To what extent is alignment being done by retraining the internal models versus,

389
00:25:06,789 --> 00:25:10,549
you know, maybe additional pipeline checks, you know, before you actually hit the model.

390
00:25:10,559 --> 00:25:13,269
Yeah.

391
00:25:13,269 --> 00:25:16,470
So again, all of these sort of processes are going to be a multi-stage thing.

392
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:21,430
You have first off the curation of the training data.

393
00:25:21,430 --> 00:25:22,950
So it's hard for a model, not impossible,

394
00:25:22,950 --> 00:25:28,230
but it's hard for a model to generalize outside of the training data.

395
00:25:28,230 --> 00:25:32,070
So if you make sure that your data is is curated to be the sort of things.

396
00:25:32,070 --> 00:25:34,310
that you want the model to reproduce, that's great.

397
00:25:34,310 --> 00:25:38,630
The second thing is reinforcement learning fine-tuning,

398
00:25:38,630 --> 00:25:42,870
which is basically where you are giving the model a reward function.

399
00:25:42,870 --> 00:25:47,510
and saying, "Hey, I'm going to reward positive behavior and I'm going to ding negative behavior."

400
00:25:47,510 --> 00:25:50,710
That's a very powerful alignment tool.

401
00:25:50,710 --> 00:25:55,669
Then what happens is after you train a model, you create a base model,

402
00:25:55,669 --> 00:26:01,669
which is basically a pool of intelligence, but it's not instruct tuned.

403
00:26:01,669 --> 00:26:04,310
So it's not necessarily ready to have a conversation yet.

404
00:26:04,310 --> 00:26:07,590
It's more just able to complete sentences.

405
00:26:07,590 --> 00:26:11,909
So you take that base model and through that instruction tuning process,

406
00:26:11,909 --> 00:26:15,990
you do additional fine-tuning to make sure that it's handling instructions correctly.

407
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:23,029
for example, you know, it's refusing to help people understand how to make chemical weapons.

408
00:26:23,029 --> 00:26:25,110
And then finally, at the deployment level,

409
00:26:25,110 --> 00:26:30,789
it is the responsibility of the developer to make sure that their models being used appropriately.

410
00:26:30,789 --> 00:26:35,990
There's a lot of new security issues and new avenues of research here.

411
00:26:35,990 --> 00:26:38,149
I think Simon Wilson's written a lot about prompt injection,

412
00:26:38,159 --> 00:26:43,510
which is the idea of like an AI system where you're able to take a a model.

