00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:25,910
But I got a B in linear algebra.

685
00:43:25,910 --> 00:43:28,950
I got a B in statistics in in college.

686
00:43:28,950 --> 00:43:31,829
Like I am good at math but not great at it.

687
00:43:31,829 --> 00:43:39,990
You don't have to be some Einstein level mathematician to grasp the mechanics of inference.

688
00:43:39,990 --> 00:43:42,710
and to understand how to make it faster.

689
00:43:42,710 --> 00:43:50,550
It's just like we talked about software engineering introducing constraints to the system and iterative experimentation and evaluation.

690
00:43:50,550 --> 00:43:52,390
So the math's important.

691
00:43:52,390 --> 00:43:54,630
It's really fun and interesting to learn.

692
00:43:54,630 --> 00:43:57,270
But as someone working in the system every day,

693
00:43:57,270 --> 00:44:00,950
I'm, you know, living proof right here on a podcast talking about it.

694
00:44:00,950 --> 00:44:05,829
that you don't have to be a PhD in math to understand how some of this stuff works.

695
00:44:05,839 --> 00:44:11,751
And as you said before, traditional software engineering challenges still apply in this context. Correct.

696
00:44:11,760 --> 00:44:14,150
Absolutely.

697
00:44:14,150 --> 00:44:20,551
Like most of AI engineering is just new versions of software engineering with some fun non-deterministic behavior sprinkled. in.

698
00:44:20,560 --> 00:44:24,390
Are there any specific aspects of software engineering.

699
00:44:24,390 --> 00:44:31,670
that really should be emphasized if you are working on self-hosting or or building multi- aent AI systems?

700
00:44:31,680 --> 00:44:39,589
I think a big one is the idea of configuration.

701
00:44:39,589 --> 00:44:42,470
So when we think about a model inference engine,

702
00:44:42,470 --> 00:44:46,470
there are hundreds of different parameters that you can adjust.

703
00:44:46,470 --> 00:44:48,950
You can adjust the quantization.

704
00:44:48,950 --> 00:44:51,829
You can adjust the speculation algorithm.

705
00:44:51,829 --> 00:44:54,710
You can adjust the batch sizes, the sequence lengths.

706
00:44:54,720 --> 00:44:56,710
There are all temperature, whatever.

707
00:44:56,720 --> 00:44:58,871
Yes, the temperature. Exactly.

708
00:44:58,871 --> 00:45:02,630
There are all of these different configurations that you can mess with.

709
00:45:02,630 --> 00:45:06,391
What's the best one? Well, it it depends.

710
00:45:06,391 --> 00:45:11,430
M so one thing that I've seen happen over the last couple years.

711
00:45:11,430 --> 00:45:14,230
and and is a trend that is continuing.

712
00:45:14,230 --> 00:45:20,791
is the idea of a lot of these configurations used to be set at deploy time. and now they're set dynamically at runtime. Mhm.

713
00:45:20,800 --> 00:45:26,150
So as we think about software infrastructure,

714
00:45:26,150 --> 00:45:30,871
a big trend over the industry for a long time has been how do we take something. that used to be static.

715
00:45:30,880 --> 00:45:37,349
and make it dynamic and make it adjust to the real world conditions it's operating under.

716
00:45:37,349 --> 00:45:41,430
The exact same thing is happening in inference engineering where we're going from,

717
00:45:41,430 --> 00:45:44,550
okay, we're going to run one benchmark for performance.

718
00:45:44,550 --> 00:45:47,670
and we're going to do as well as we can on that benchmark.

719
00:45:47,670 --> 00:45:51,670
and then we're just going to roll out this system to having a system.

720
00:45:51,670 --> 00:45:54,150
that automatically adjusts to scale to the nature of the traffic.

721
00:45:54,150 --> 00:45:56,150
and to the performance it's seeing.

722
00:45:56,150 --> 00:45:58,710
in order to just continually improve.

723
00:45:58,720 --> 00:46:01,829
Well, that speaks to the importance of observability.

724
00:46:01,839 --> 00:46:07,190
Can you speak some to how are companies doing observability in this space to ensure.

725
00:46:07,190 --> 00:46:08,870
that they're getting good results out of these systems?

726
00:46:08,880 --> 00:46:12,710
We use all the standard observability tools.

727
00:46:12,710 --> 00:46:16,870
We have our our graphana dashboards and our sentry alerts and stuff.

728
00:46:16,870 --> 00:46:21,910
Again, if these models are going to be core missionritical parts of your software stack,

729
00:46:21,910 --> 00:46:25,190
they need to be treated like core mission critical parts of your software stack.

730
00:46:25,190 --> 00:46:25,191
[laughter]

731
00:46:25,191 --> 00:46:27,270
You should get paged when latencies elevate.

732
00:46:27,270 --> 00:46:29,750
You should get paged when GPU nodes cycle.

733
00:46:29,760 --> 00:46:34,309
So, a big part of our observability is around the performance.

734
00:46:34,309 --> 00:46:36,470
Just making sure that we're keeping an eye on traffic,

735
00:46:36,480 --> 00:46:40,630
keeping an eye on latencies, keeping those steady, keeping an eye on errors.

736
00:46:40,640 --> 00:46:46,630
making sure that like there aren't too many requests timing out, that kind of stuff.

737
00:46:46,630 --> 00:46:51,190
And there's also the AI observability layer in terms of the model outputs.

738
00:46:51,190 --> 00:46:53,829
and and making sure that those are continuously of high quality.

739
00:46:53,839 --> 00:46:56,710
I have a lot less experience there being.

740
00:46:56,720 --> 00:47:00,550
that I work more on the infrastructure side than on the AI engineering side.

741
00:47:00,560 --> 00:47:07,030
But from my understanding, it's basically keeping an eye on those eval numbers,

742
00:47:07,030 --> 00:47:08,870
making sure that they're trending in the right direction.

743
00:47:08,870 --> 00:47:10,870
and also just listening to your users,

744
00:47:10,870 --> 00:47:13,190
like just having open lines of communication,

745
00:47:13,200 --> 00:47:19,030
making sure that users are continuously happy with the service that they're getting from the models.

746
00:47:19,030 --> 00:47:20,710
What other responsibilities do you have to take on.

747
00:47:20,720 --> 00:47:25,349
when you either host your own models or you're building multi- aent systems?

748
00:47:25,359 --> 00:47:32,390
So GPUs are great technology like I would not have this career in in inference engineering without the.

749
00:47:32,400 --> 00:47:34,550
existence of GPUs.

750
00:47:34,550 --> 00:47:36,550
So I'm very thankful for them.

751
00:47:36,550 --> 00:47:39,109
But they're also kind of a pain to work with sometimes.

752
00:47:39,109 --> 00:47:45,829
If anyone's ever run a long running task on GPUs in production and in a data center,

753
00:47:45,829 --> 00:47:51,910
they know that GPUs do not stay up 99.99% of the time.

754
00:47:51,910 --> 00:47:56,470
But if nothing close to that, like you're lucky to have a couple nines in there.

755
00:47:56,470 --> 00:47:59,910
But if you are building say a assistant for doctors.

756
00:47:59,910 --> 00:48:04,550
that every single doctor in America is going to use to make sure.

757
00:48:04,550 --> 00:48:06,230
that they're delivering the best possible care.

758
00:48:06,230 --> 00:48:09,109
which is something that one of our customers has built.

759
00:48:09,109 --> 00:48:14,230
then you need to have that extremely high reliability.

760
00:48:14,230 --> 00:48:19,270
because if you're interfering with a medical process that that can literally be life or death.

761
00:48:19,270 --> 00:48:24,230
And so the big responsibility that you have on the inference engineering.

762
00:48:24,230 --> 00:48:32,630
and infrastructure side that you're taking on when you switch to models that you're running yourself.

763
00:48:32,630 --> 00:48:34,950
is to go from these very flaky.

764
00:48:34,950 --> 00:48:40,950
and unreliable hardware systems into a software layer that orchestrates all this.

765
00:48:40,950 --> 00:48:43,349
Has active active reliability.

766
00:48:43,349 --> 00:48:47,270
Goes in multiple clouds just in case one of your cloud providers goes down.

767
00:48:47,270 --> 00:48:52,710
goes in multiple regions so you're close to every user can fail over from cluster to cluster can.

768
00:48:52,710 --> 00:48:56,470
cycle nodes automatically when they have issues.

769
00:48:56,470 --> 00:49:03,510
this software orchestration layer becomes your responsibility as well and is very very critical.

770
00:49:03,510 --> 00:49:07,510
because reliability is one of the differentiators today in the AI industry.

771
00:49:07,510 --> 00:49:12,549
everyone complains when chat GPT goes down everyone's always asking on Twitter like hey does this model seem.

772
00:49:12,559 --> 00:49:17,109
dumb today or you know what's going on under the hood.

773
00:49:17,109 --> 00:49:20,230
put in the system when you take.

774
00:49:20,230 --> 00:49:22,309
that in-house you do gain control over all of.

775
00:49:22,309 --> 00:49:24,549
that but again you also gain all of that responsibility.

776
00:49:24,549 --> 00:49:28,710
and depending what you're working on that can be a very very heavy responsibility.

777
00:49:28,720 --> 00:49:32,230
I mean everything you just said about orchestration across multiple regions.

778
00:49:32,240 --> 00:49:37,589
and failover and high availability that still just sounds like software engineering.

779
00:49:37,599 --> 00:49:41,750
it's we can't just sprinkle AI on it Philip and just have it work.

780
00:49:41,760 --> 00:49:48,309
there's a reason they let me on software engineering radio instead of having to create AI engineering radio.

781
00:49:48,309 --> 00:49:53,990
It's a field that I think should be very welcoming to everyone with a background in any kind.

782
00:49:53,990 --> 00:49:56,230
of software engineering work because it's all needed.

783
00:49:56,230 --> 00:50:00,630
Again, we got to do the same stuff that we've done for all of our previous systems.

784
00:50:00,630 --> 00:50:02,790
We have to adapt it.

785
00:50:02,790 --> 00:50:07,670
Of course, the systems are bigger, they're faster, they're less deterministic.

786
00:50:07,670 --> 00:50:11,349
but we have to do a lot of the same engineering work as an industry to make these.

787
00:50:11,349 --> 00:50:14,069
things grow and scale.

788
00:50:14,069 --> 00:50:17,030
that we did in every single one of the last generations of technology.

789
00:50:17,040 --> 00:50:19,589
Yeah.

790
00:50:19,589 --> 00:50:21,430
So, let's say someone wants to start transitioning.

791
00:50:21,430 --> 00:50:25,510
they've been adding chat bots on top of their existing systems.

792
00:50:25,510 --> 00:50:28,870
and now they're listening to this show or they've been you know reading other things.

793
00:50:28,870 --> 00:50:31,190
and they're saying I want to get into this agentic world.

794
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:35,750
and I want to start looking even at multi- aent AI.

795
00:50:35,750 --> 00:50:40,470
what should they do to get started or what approaches might they take?

796
00:50:40,470 --> 00:50:43,349
I would really recommend an approach of iterative experimentation.

797
00:50:43,349 --> 00:50:47,829
I think the easiest way for this to go wrong is to decide.

798
00:50:47,829 --> 00:50:52,549
that you want to build the single greatest AI AI system to ever exist.

799
00:50:52,549 --> 00:50:54,630
and you're going to do it today.

800
00:50:54,630 --> 00:50:56,870
Obviously, you should have a lot of ambition working in this space.

801
00:50:56,870 --> 00:50:58,630
This space is growing fast.

802
00:50:58,630 --> 00:51:01,109
People are building unbelievable things.

803
00:51:01,109 --> 00:51:06,231
But it's important to stay grounded in the day-to-day work of how can I make something a little. bit better today.

804
00:51:06,240 --> 00:51:12,309
That might be introducing a new modality, saying like, okay, I'm going to add voice to my chat.

805
00:51:12,309 --> 00:51:14,790
It might be introducing new context, saying,

806
00:51:14,790 --> 00:51:19,910
I'm going to give my agent access to a new type of data that it hasn't had before.

807
00:51:19,920 --> 00:51:26,069
And as we've been talking about, it could be something entirely unrelated to AI.

808
00:51:26,069 --> 00:51:28,470
You could be making better tools for agents to use.

809
00:51:28,470 --> 00:51:33,990
You could be cleaning up your docs so that it's easier to find out what information to pull.

810
00:51:33,990 --> 00:51:38,870
You could be optimizing the latency of your networking stack.

811
00:51:38,870 --> 00:51:44,151
so that you're not having users hang out for 50 milliseconds every time they want to make a. call.

812
00:51:44,151 --> 00:51:49,109
There's so much engineering work to do around making AI systems better.

813
00:51:49,109 --> 00:51:51,670
and more reliable both within and outside of AI engineering.

814
00:51:51,670 --> 00:51:54,470
that there's some place for everyone to contribute.

